Trigonometric bridge (B.1 calibration, elementary): for
$\theta \in (0, \pi/2)$ satisfying the calibration
$a = R \sin\theta - (R/2)\sin(2\theta)$,
\[
\frac{R}{2a} = \frac{1}{1 - \cos\theta} .
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Substitute the calibration and factor:
$2a = 2R\sin\theta - R\sin(2\theta)
= 2R\sin\theta\,(1 - \cos\theta)$ by the double-angle identity
$\sin(2\theta) = 2\sin\theta\cos\theta$.  Since $\theta \in (0, \pi/2)$,
$\sin\theta \ne 0$, so cancelling $R\sin\theta$ against the numerator $R$
leaves $R/(2a) = 1/(1 - \cos\theta)$.
\end{proof}

